\description{
Computes pairs (case) bootstrap standard errors and confidence intervals for
the coefficients of an ordinary least squares model fitted with
\code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}. The pairs bootstrap resamples the observations
\eqn{(y_t, x_t)} with replacement, refits OLS on each resample, and
summarizes the resulting sampling distribution of \eqn{\hat\beta}. It makes no
assumption about the form of the error variance, so it is a useful empirical
reference for the analytic heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors
produced by \code{\link[=hcinfer]{hcinfer()}} and \code{\link[=vcov_hc]{vcov_hc()}}.
}
\details{
For each of \code{R} bootstrap replicates, a sample of \code{n} row indices is drawn
with replacement from \verb{1, ..., n}, and OLS is refitted on the resampled rows
\eqn{(y_{i}, x_{i})}. Writing \eqn{\hat\beta^{*}_{(r)}} for the estimate on
replicate \code{r}, the bootstrap standard error of coefficient \code{j} is the sample
standard deviation of \eqn{\hat\beta^{*}_{1,j}, \ldots, \hat\beta^{*}_{R,j}},
and the bias estimate is the mean of the replicates minus the original OLS
estimate.

Three interval types are available through \code{ci_type}. Let
\eqn{q_\alpha} denote the empirical \eqn{\alpha} quantile of the bootstrap
replicates for a coefficient, \eqn{\hat\beta_j} the original estimate,
\eqn{s^{*}_j} the bootstrap standard error, and
\eqn{\alpha = 1 - \texttt{level}}.
The \code{"percentile"} interval is
\eqn{[q_{\alpha/2}, q_{1-\alpha/2}]}. The \code{"basic"} (reverse percentile)
interval is
\eqn{[2\hat\beta_j - q_{1-\alpha/2}, \; 2\hat\beta_j - q_{\alpha/2}]}. The
\code{"normal"} interval is
\eqn{\hat\beta_j \pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\, s^{*}_j}, with \eqn{z} the standard
normal quantile.

\strong{Reproducibility.} When \code{seed} is supplied, all resampling indices are drawn
once, sequentially, in the main process under that seed, and the per-replicate
fit is deterministic. The results are therefore identical whether the run is
sequential or parallel and regardless of the number of cores. The caller's
random number generator state is saved and restored, so calling \code{boot_pairs()}
does not disturb a surrounding random stream. When \code{seed} is \code{NULL}, the
current RNG state is used and results are not reproducible.

\strong{Parallelism.} With \code{parallel = TRUE}, the deterministic per-replicate fits
are distributed with \code{\link[purrr:in_parallel]{purrr::in_parallel()}}, which uses the \pkg{mirai}
package as its backend. \code{boot_pairs()} starts \code{cores} daemons for the duration
of the call and shuts them down on exit; do not call it while relying on
externally configured \code{mirai} daemons. Parallelism only speeds up the
computation: it never changes the numeric result. It is worthwhile mainly for
large \code{R} or large \code{n}; for small problems the setup overhead can dominate.

\strong{Rank-deficient resamples.} A resample can be rank deficient (for example
when a resample omits the observations that identify a coefficient). Such
replicates are dropped, a warning reports how many were dropped, and the
summaries use the remaining replicates. The call errors if fewer than two
valid replicates remain.
